From 07c9e397aad6c7a37089cae1fc241627050551ad Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: snarayana <snarayana@student.ethz.ch>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 07:03:05 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 1/3] feat: a checkpoint fault injector, to prove verify
 catches what it must

`servekit verify` exists because sharded_state has corrupted models
silently (#14 / sglang#34448, #35702), but nothing shows it would catch
the next such bug. `python -m servekit.faults` writes a corrupted copy of
a safetensors checkpoint at a chosen magnitude, so a check can be tested
against known-bad loads as well as known-good ones:

  zero_slice / dup_slice / permute_shards / transpose   TP shard layout
  bitflip                       any bit position, any dtype (BF16, FP8, F32)
  drop_tensor                   a tensor missing from files and index
  scale_blocks                  FP8 weight_scale_inv rolled, set to 1,
                                jittered, transposed, from the other shard
  mxfp4_scales                  E8M0 exponents shifted, rolled, zeroed
  swap_experts                  per-expert or fused (gpt-oss) MoE tensors
  noise / scale                 smooth magnitude knobs

It works on raw bytes through numpy views (BF16 as uint16, FP8 as
uint8), so every dtype round-trips exactly and torch is not needed;
only the shards it touches are rewritten, the rest are hard-linked, and
servekit-fault.json records the ground truth. `validate` checks a fault
against names and shapes alone, e.g. a remote checkpoint's headers.
numpy is an optional extra (`servekit[faults]`).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
